   "grammarNotes": [
    {
     "title": "は and が",
     "paragraphs": [
      "は and が have no direct English meaning, but they are important grammar tools in Japanese. You can recognise which one to use through English cues.",
      "There is / there are → use が, in the pattern Place に item が あります／います。",
      "The item/thing is → use は, in the pattern Item は place に あります／います。"
     ],
     "examples": [
      {
       "jp": "いまに テレビが あります。",
       "en": "There is a TV in the living room."
      },
      {
       "jp": "にわに ねこが います。",
       "en": "There is a cat in the garden."
      },
      {
       "jp": "テレビは いまに あります。",
       "en": "The TV is in the living room."
      },
      {
       "jp": "ねこは にわに います。",
       "en": "The cat is in the garden."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "title": "Particle panic! と vs や",
     "paragraphs": [
      "と and や both mean 'and', but the difference is important: と means you have to name all the items, while や means you only have to name some of them.",
      "など (etc.) is optional after や."
     ],
     "examples": [
      {
       "jp": "テーブルの 上に ペンと えんぴつと 本と けしごむと ものさしと ふでばこが あります。",
       "en": "On the table there are pens, pencils, a book, an eraser, a ruler and a pencil case (a complete list)."
      },
      {
       "jp": "テーブルの 上に ペンや けしごむや ものさし などが あります。",
       "en": "On the table there are pens, an eraser, a ruler, etc. (a partial list)."
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
